# Backend Security

- Treat HTTP input, headers, tokens, and message payloads as untrusted.
- Validate and normalize input before use; never interpolate untrusted input into queries.
- Keep secrets in environment variables; never hard-code passwords, JWT secrets, or LLM keys.
- Authenticate before authorization; enforce ownership checks for user-scoped resources.
- Return safe client errors and avoid exposing stack traces, credentials, or internal database details.
- Add rate limiting and restrictive CORS deliberately when public endpoints are introduced.
